  "unit_key": "g3-sec2-g2",
  "position": 3,
  "status": "draft",
  "title": "Properties of similar triangles and polygons",
  "summary": "Use equal corresponding angles and proportional corresponding sides to solve geometric problems.",
  "outcomes": [
    "2.3"
  ],
  "prerequisite_lessons": [
    "g2-lesson-02"
  ],
  "estimated_minutes": 55,
  "objectives": [
    "State a correct correspondence between vertices.",
    "Form and solve proportions from corresponding sides.",
    "Use angle sums and similarity properties together."
  ],
  "mastery_policy_key": "g2-default-v1",
  "sections": [
    {
      "stable_key": "g2-23-method",
      "position": 1,
      "type": "explanation",
      "title": "Core method",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The order of vertices fixes the correspondence. If ABC is similar to PQR, then A matches P, B matches Q and C matches R. Ratios must keep that order."
        },
        {
          "type": "display_math",
          "latex": "\\frac{AB}{PQ}=\\frac{BC}{QR}=\\frac{AC}{PR}"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "g2-lesson-03-visual",
      "position": 2,
      "type": "explanation",
      "title": "Read the diagram",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "A similarity statement such as triangle ABC similar to triangle PQR fixes A↔P, B↔Q and C↔R, so the corresponding sides are AB↔PQ, BC↔QR and AC↔PR. The diagram shows a common case: D is on AB, E is on AC and DE is parallel to BC. Triangles ADE and ABC share angle A, and the parallel lines make their other corresponding angles equal, so the small triangle ADE is similar to the whole triangle ABC with A↔A, D↔B and E↔C."
        },
        {
          "type": "asset_ref",
          "asset_key": "g2-lesson-03-diagram"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "g2-lesson-03-reasoning",
      "position": 3,
      "type": "explanation",
      "title": "Reasoning method",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Use ratios with matching orientation, such as AB/PQ=BC/QR. Cross-multiplication then solves an unknown side. Equal corresponding angles can establish AA similarity; once similarity is known, every corresponding length follows the same scale factor."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "g2-23-example",
      "position": 4,
      "type": "worked_example",
      "title": "Worked example: a triangle inside a triangle",
      "prompt": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "In the diagram, DE is parallel to BC, AD = 5 cm, AB = 15 cm and DE = 6 cm. Find BC, which is marked y cm."
        }
      ],
      "steps": [
        {
          "position": 1,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Triangle ADE is similar to triangle ABC, with AD↔AB and DE↔BC. Use the whole side AB, not DB."
            },
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "k=\\frac{AB}{AD}=\\frac{15}{5}=3"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "position": 2,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "y=BC=3\\times DE=3\\times6=18"
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "verification_note": "The ratios agree: 15/5 = 18/6 = 3. Using DB = 10 cm instead of AB would give the wrong factor 2."
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "g2-lesson-03-example-2",
      "position": 5,
      "type": "worked_example",
      "title": "Worked example: two unknown sides",
      "prompt": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Triangles ABC and PQR are similar in that order. AB = 8 cm, BC = 12 cm, PQ = 14 cm and PR = 21 cm. Find QR and AC."
        }
      ],
      "steps": [
        {
          "position": 1,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The scale factor from ABC to PQR is"
            },
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "\\frac{PQ}{AB}=\\frac{14}{8}=\\frac74"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "position": 2,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "QR=BC\\times\\frac74=12\\times\\frac74=21\\text{ cm}"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "position": 3,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "AC corresponds to PR, so divide by the factor."
            },
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "AC=PR\\div\\frac74=21\\times\\frac47=12\\text{ cm}"
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "verification_note": "The three ratios PQ/AB = 14/8, QR/BC = 21/12 and PR/AC = 21/12 all equal 7/4."
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "g2-lesson-03-pitfalls",
      "position": 6,
      "type": "explanation",
      "title": "Common errors and checks",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Do not pair sides because they occupy a similar place on the page. Use vertex order or equal-angle markings. Avoid reversing only one ratio; write a complete correspondence line before calculating."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "g2-23-recall",
      "position": 7,
      "type": "active_recall",
      "title": "Check your understanding",
      "prompt": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Two corresponding sides are 4 cm and 10 cm. Find the scale factor from the first figure to the second."
        }
      ],
      "response": {
        "type": "numeric",
        "comparison_mode": "exact_numeric",
        "canonical_answer": "2.5",
        "canonical_latex": "2.5",
        "accepted_answers": [],
        "absolute_tolerance": null,
        "rounding_precision": null,
        "unit": null
      },
      "feedback": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Divide the image side by its corresponding original: 10 ÷ 4 = 2.5."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "g2-lesson-03-summary",
      "position": 8,
      "type": "summary",
      "title": "Lesson checklist",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The order of the vertices in a similarity statement fixes which sides correspond. All pairs of corresponding sides have the same ratio."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Write the correspondence, find the scale factor from one known pair, then multiply or divide by it. When one triangle sits inside another, use the whole side of the large triangle."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
